    {
      "id": "M4169-A",
      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
      "language": "en",
      "prompt": "Request A: A hypothetical industrial-quality estimator makes 100 stochastic forward passes for one input. Exactly 50 latent-mean predictions equal -2 and 50 equal +2, in quality units. Its specified independent observation-noise variance is 5. Authors use a Gaussian moment approximation and call mean +/- 1.645 times predictive SD an approximate 90% output prediction interval. They propose dividing this SD by sqrt(100), describe it as a 90% confidence interval on the true quality, and claim 90% reliability for every operating condition because an untouched 100-target test set has 90 covered targets. The ten missed targets all belong to a ten-target minority condition. Without running experiments, compute the variance, SD and approximate bounds under the stated moment approximation; distinguish the output target, MC estimation error, conditional versus aggregate coverage, and what additional records or actual study would be needed. Do not assert the Gaussian approximation is an exact interval for a mixture. Also inspect whether the current Skill has source-grounded output-interval guidance and matched source/Chinese-name records; absence is not invalidity. ",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Keep predictive variance 9, SD 3 and approximate bounds +/-4.935 distinct from MC mean-estimation error; do not infer exact mixture quantiles or condition-wise guarantees from 90/100 overall and 0/10 minority coverage.",
        "Read scoped P103/P104 quantity/use records and separate Chinese wording evidence from algorithm admission and unresolved source formulas."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Divide predictive SD by sqrt(100) to claim an individual output interval or invent latent-noise coverage, a full interval-method pair or a safety guarantee.",
        "Turn missing admission into invalidity, repair real manuscripts/results, or run experiments."
      ],
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "reason": "Separates predictive variance/spread, numerical mean error and run-level graphics; preserves approximate bounds and aggregate/minority coverage, and reads source/Chinese wording records without full algorithm or safety certification.",
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review; no independent second grader",
      "response_trace": "responses/v4169-independent.md",
      "review_trace": "reviews/v4169-independent-evaluation.md",
      "evaluation_phase": "one_new_context_two_unchanged_requests_on_final_snapshot"
    },
    {
      "id": "M4169-B",
      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
      "language": "en",
      "prompt": "Request B: Give three concise reusable English prose blocks only, then separate short review notes: (1) a 35-55-word method/abstract chain connecting process measurements, an estimated quality distribution, interval endpoints and a declared warning rule, with no invented architecture; (2) a 20-40-word contribution linking the same mechanism to an evaluable engineering output without a novelty or safety guarantee; (3) a 20-40-word result/interpretation preserving the given 90/100 aggregate coverage and 0/10 minority-condition coverage, with no invented performance comparisons. These are synthetic facts, not a real experiment. State word/sentence counts. Keep intervals distinct from run-to-run error bars and latent-variable variances; do not require this design in all soft-sensor papers.",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Write positive 35-55/20-40/20-40-word prose blocks connecting estimated quality, interval endpoints and a declared decision, with any added warning example labeled as illustrative.",
        "Retain supplied aggregate/minority outcomes and keep novelty, calibration assumptions and actual study needs in separate notes."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Invent an architecture, safety gain, warning performance, superiority or a mandatory interval branch for every soft sensor.",
        "Treat run-level graphics or arbitrary latent variance as a calibrated output interval."
      ],
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "reason": "Connects quality distribution, declared interval endpoints and decision use in positive prose; preserves supplied coverage outcomes and unverified novelty, with any added rule identified as illustrative rather than validated performance.",
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review; no independent second grader",
